1045
00:49:32.180 --> 00:49:32.980
I mean, it's like.

1046
00:49:33.100 --> 00:49:33.360
I know.

1047
00:49:33.480 --> 00:49:35.220
He's like, and then what do you do with them?

1048
00:49:35.380 --> 00:49:39.640
And she goes, are you really asking me what I do with the checks that people give me?

1049
00:49:39.920 --> 00:49:42.360
And he's like, I'm just, I'm trying to make conversation, okay?

1050
00:49:42.960 --> 00:49:43.680
Oh, Michael.

1051
00:49:43.680 --> 00:49:47.820
And he just talks about like, he goes, well, this is who I was dating before you.

1052
00:49:48.020 --> 00:49:50.020
And he shows the photo of Jan.

1053
00:49:50.400 --> 00:49:52.620
Oh, and then he calls her by accident.

1054
00:49:52.620 --> 00:49:54.600
It's just like layer of layer of cringy.

1055
00:49:54.700 --> 00:49:59.840
And then he kind of starts opening up to Margaret about like how it's so hard to find someone.

1056
00:50:00.020 --> 00:50:01.660
And he tells her, you know, thanks.

1057
00:50:01.880 --> 00:50:02.620
This has been nice.

1058
00:50:02.740 --> 00:50:05.000
It's like talking to the old lady on the bus.

1059
00:50:05.520 --> 00:50:06.080
Mm-hmm.

1060
00:50:06.440 --> 00:50:07.600
And Margaret's had it.

1061
00:50:07.920 --> 00:50:08.900
She's like, you're rude.

1062
00:50:09.020 --> 00:50:09.660
That was rude.

1063
00:50:10.080 --> 00:50:12.220
Well, and then Michael says, well, now you've ruined it.

1064
00:50:12.220 --> 00:50:14.500
But Michael was such a jerk in this scene.

1065
00:50:15.020 --> 00:50:21.220
Listen, if you had any sympathy for him after dinner party, it evaporated after this scene.

1066
00:50:21.640 --> 00:50:21.820
Yeah.

1067
00:50:21.960 --> 00:50:28.920
We were very good at taking you on quite the roller coaster as your feelings for Michael are concerned on this show.

1068
00:50:29.100 --> 00:50:31.500
But at this scene, I was like, you deserve Jan.

1069
00:50:31.720 --> 00:50:33.980
You deserve to sleep on the bench at the foot of the bed.

1070
00:50:34.080 --> 00:50:35.180
You're being such a turd.

1071
00:50:35.480 --> 00:50:35.920
Exactly.

1072
00:50:35.920 --> 00:50:37.220
It's very complicated.

1073
00:50:37.220 --> 00:50:45.460
Well, now we go into this wonderful scene where Andy and Kevin are going to meet with the heads of the five families.

1074
00:50:45.860 --> 00:50:46.980
Make their plea.

1075
00:50:47.560 --> 00:50:51.580
They're in what looks like to be some kind of conference room at the Scranton Business Park.

1076
00:50:51.580 --> 00:50:59.040
I wanted to point out at 14 minutes, 12 seconds, how nice the freaking TV is in this conference room.

1077
00:50:59.220 --> 00:50:59.560
Yeah.

1078
00:50:59.760 --> 00:51:02.600
It is a true big flat screen TV.

1079
00:51:03.000 --> 00:51:05.080
Not like Michael's tiny one.

1080
00:51:05.300 --> 00:51:12.900
Nothing like our crazy old tube TV that we roll in on a cart for our conference room scenes.

1081
00:51:13.200 --> 00:51:13.420
Yeah.

1082
00:51:13.420 --> 00:51:15.700
It's like a fancy big flat screen TV.

1083
00:51:16.300 --> 00:51:16.740
Yeah.

1084
00:51:17.400 --> 00:51:18.980
Imagine if Michael had seen it.

1085
00:51:19.080 --> 00:51:20.000
He would have passed out.

1086
00:51:20.000 --> 00:51:25.120
Well, we asked Paul about shooting this scene, and I loved what he had to say.

1087
00:51:25.360 --> 00:51:26.060
Here it is.

1088
00:51:26.720 --> 00:51:30.720
So being on set, and sort of be careful what you wish for, because I'm a huge fan of the show.

1089
00:51:30.900 --> 00:51:31.860
I think they're super funny.

1090
00:51:32.020 --> 00:51:37.300
And I remember the first time we're about to shoot, they say, okay, quiet on the set.

1091
00:51:37.820 --> 00:51:38.680
Camera's rolling.

1092
00:51:38.920 --> 00:51:40.400
And I will never forget it.

1093
00:51:40.520 --> 00:51:44.740
But Brian says, okay, nobody F up.

1094
00:51:45.000 --> 00:51:47.620
And he looks at me.

1095
00:51:47.980 --> 00:51:51.340
And, you know, he was messing around, but it was so funny.

1096
00:51:51.340 --> 00:51:57.180
And then the entire time we shot the scene, I had my lines to say, but we shot it about 20, 30 times.

1097
00:51:57.500 --> 00:52:01.360
And every single time, Brian and Ed said different things.

1098
00:52:01.360 --> 00:52:03.320
And I happen to think they're super funny.

1099
00:52:03.460 --> 00:52:05.680
And it was very hard to keep a straight face.

1100
00:52:05.840 --> 00:52:07.840
I never knew what was going to come out of their mouth.

1101
00:52:08.080 --> 00:52:10.660
They were making faces at me, trying to get me to laugh.

1102
00:52:10.660 --> 00:52:12.640
And I had to sit there and be dead serious.

1103
00:52:12.820 --> 00:52:14.640
I didn't want to be the guy to mess up the scene.

1104
00:52:14.960 --> 00:52:20.180
So working with people who were literally comic geniuses in their delivery and their timing,

1105
00:52:20.320 --> 00:52:26.160
having to sit there and being the new guy on set and trying to keep a straight face and not to mess up was a challenge.

1106
00:52:26.340 --> 00:52:29.880
But such a life experience.

1107
00:52:30.000 --> 00:52:31.460
And just so funny to do.

1108
00:52:31.460 --> 00:52:34.520
And I'm so honored to be a part of a small little part of the office.

1109
00:52:35.340 --> 00:52:37.580
I love that Brian and Ed were trying to mess with them.

1110
00:52:37.720 --> 00:52:38.540
We would do this.

1111
00:52:38.760 --> 00:52:41.880
We would know when someone was going to break and we would have a little fun.

1112
00:52:42.140 --> 00:52:42.660
We would.

1113
00:52:42.800 --> 00:52:43.280
It's true.

1114
00:52:43.700 --> 00:52:47.460
Well, Paul mentioned to us, you know, what it was like to get lines at the last minute.

1115
00:52:47.500 --> 00:52:52.160
But he also talks to us about what it was like just being on the set and the lingo that they used.

1116
00:52:52.700 --> 00:52:55.360
So we're shooting the scene over and over again.

1117
00:52:55.520 --> 00:52:56.440
You know, we want to get it right.

1118
00:52:56.440 --> 00:52:59.340
And sometimes the lines don't work, but the facial expressions are great.

1119
00:52:59.620 --> 00:53:04.160
So one of the guys comes in and says, when this happens, I forget the word used.

1120
00:53:04.300 --> 00:53:05.160
I want you to do this.

1121
00:53:05.300 --> 00:53:09.840
And then he walked out and I'm looking around like, I have no idea what that means.

1122
00:53:10.100 --> 00:53:13.620
So the camera guy behind me, he's like, you don't know what that means?

1123
00:53:13.720 --> 00:53:15.620
I'm like, no, I've never done this before.

1124
00:53:15.840 --> 00:53:19.700
He goes, OK, I'll tap you on the shoulder when you should say your line.

1125
00:53:19.700 --> 00:53:22.280
So I was literally sitting there and something was going on.

1126
00:53:22.420 --> 00:53:26.300
And I felt the camera guy who was shooting over my shoulder tap me on the shoulder when

1127
00:53:26.300 --> 00:53:27.060
to say my line.

1128
00:53:27.320 --> 00:53:31.020
And it was just sort of indicative of the whole experience because everybody was so friendly

1129
00:53:31.020 --> 00:53:33.280
and helpful and just wanted to get it done right.

1130
00:53:34.140 --> 00:53:38.260
Except, I think, Brian and Ed, who were just trying to make me laugh the whole time.

1131
00:53:39.180 --> 00:53:43.720
Can I say, Angela, that when we got these audio clips from Paul, the thing that was so fun

1132
00:53:43.720 --> 00:53:52.560
for me was his observations as a non-actor being on a set, because it really took me back

1133
00:53:52.560 --> 00:53:58.480
to all of those moments in my career as an actor where you just are learning on the fly.

1134
00:53:58.740 --> 00:54:03.900
I didn't learn any of this stuff in theater school, and I don't even know if there is any

1135
00:54:03.900 --> 00:54:04.720
other way to learn it.

1136
00:54:04.780 --> 00:54:06.360
You just kind of absorb it in the moment.

1137
00:54:06.380 --> 00:54:09.460
But he sent in an audio clip that is my favorite.

1138
00:54:09.720 --> 00:54:10.280
Me too.

1139
00:54:10.500 --> 00:54:12.720
Because I struggled with this too.

1140
00:54:13.660 --> 00:54:17.100
You and I have talked about this, but I don't think we've ever talked about it on the pod.

1141
00:54:17.280 --> 00:54:17.980
So this is great.

1142
00:54:18.220 --> 00:54:21.200
I love that he had the same observation.

1143
00:54:21.980 --> 00:54:23.900
Okay, we'll play it, and then we'll talk about it.

1144
00:54:24.280 --> 00:54:25.780
So this was a new experience for me.

1145
00:54:26.020 --> 00:54:29.940
I kind of, it was my first time being really on the set, and I'm sitting in my trailer

1146
00:54:29.940 --> 00:54:31.220
going, what do I do now?

1147
00:54:31.280 --> 00:54:32.420
So I decided to get something to eat.

1148
00:54:32.780 --> 00:54:35.260
I walk out, and I'm like, where can I get some food?

1149
00:54:35.280 --> 00:54:37.600
And they pointed me in the direction of where the breakfast was.

1150
00:54:37.860 --> 00:54:42.220
And as I started walking, I heard someone in the headset go, cool guy, Paul's going to breakfaSt.

1151
00:54:42.680 --> 00:54:45.600
And then I come back, and I hear them go, cool guy, Paul's back in his trailer.

1152
00:54:46.060 --> 00:54:48.380
Everywhere I went, someone's like, cool guy, Paul's in the bathroom.

1153
00:54:48.860 --> 00:54:49.760
Cool guy, Paul's in it.

1154
00:54:49.980 --> 00:54:51.800
Like, someone had to know where I was at all times.

1155
00:54:51.880 --> 00:54:52.580
I thought it was really funny.

1156
00:54:52.720 --> 00:54:55.840
They had to track me to make sure they knew where I was in case they needed me.

1157
00:54:56.100 --> 00:54:59.480
So that was one super funny story from being on the set.

1158
00:54:59.480 --> 00:55:07.680
So yeah, you guys, on film and television sets, there is a whole network of production

1159
00:55:07.680 --> 00:55:12.080
assistants that wear these walkie-talkies, and they have little earpieces, kind of like

1160
00:55:12.080 --> 00:55:13.860
the Secret Service, you know?

1161
00:55:13.860 --> 00:55:15.380
But not as fancy.

1162
00:55:15.680 --> 00:55:16.640
But not as fancy.

1163
00:55:16.920 --> 00:55:19.040
Like, when they talk into them, you hear it echo.

1164
00:55:21.120 --> 00:55:25.700
Well, their job is literally to keep track of all the actors at all times.

1165
00:55:26.220 --> 00:55:30.640
And you can hear them tracking you all day as you walk by.

1166
00:55:30.880 --> 00:55:35.560
As you walk by, and sometimes you hear it from the person directly behind you who's actually

1167
00:55:35.560 --> 00:55:36.500
speaking about you.

1168
00:55:36.620 --> 00:55:41.960
And then you hear it in stereo from the other person they're talking to as you walk past them.

1169
00:55:41.960 --> 00:55:46.080
And I guess the theory behind it, Jenna, is that production needs to know where we are,

1170
00:55:46.160 --> 00:55:50.200
because if the scene is lit and ready, they need us on that soundstage, ready to go.

1171
00:55:50.380 --> 00:55:54.240
And they can't have an actor unaccounted for, because then that holds everyone up and it

1172
00:55:54.240 --> 00:55:55.220
costs money, right?

1173
00:55:55.520 --> 00:55:55.760
Yeah.

1174
00:55:55.900 --> 00:55:58.580
So when they're lighting a scene, they let you go back to your trailer.

1175
00:55:58.920 --> 00:55:59.960
You might use the restroom.

1176
00:56:00.080 --> 00:56:00.940
You might get a snack.

1177
00:56:01.080 --> 00:56:04.780
In our case, maybe we pop over to the writer's building and we're hanging out.

1178
00:56:05.100 --> 00:56:08.660
But once the scene is ready, they have no time to lose.

1179
00:56:08.660 --> 00:56:12.840
Because a production day is very regimented.

1180
00:56:12.960 --> 00:56:16.400
And if you run out of time, that means you probably have to cut something.

1181
00:56:16.840 --> 00:56:18.600
And you don't want that, right?

1182
00:56:18.760 --> 00:56:20.460
You don't want to have to cut content.

1183
00:56:20.920 --> 00:56:21.080
Right.

1184
00:56:21.260 --> 00:56:23.460
So yeah, all day long.

1185
00:56:23.640 --> 00:56:29.020
But my favorite thing, Angela, is that they came up with a code for the bathroom.

1186
00:56:29.020 --> 00:56:32.620
I guess maybe it's to be, I don't know, more discreet.

1187
00:56:32.800 --> 00:56:34.960
But everyone knows what the code is.

1188
00:56:35.080 --> 00:56:36.820
The code is 10-1.

1189
00:56:37.520 --> 00:56:38.480
Jenna is 10-1.

1190
00:56:38.660 --> 00:56:41.720
Everyone knows when you go to the bathroom on a set.

1191
00:56:42.420 --> 00:56:43.900
Every single person.

1192
00:56:43.900 --> 00:56:46.620
It goes through every walkie-talkie.

1193
00:56:47.140 --> 00:56:47.980
Angela 10-1.

1194
00:56:48.260 --> 00:56:48.940
Jenna 10-1.

1195
00:56:49.480 --> 00:56:52.660
And I just want you to know, what if you need to 10-2?

1196
00:56:53.640 --> 00:56:55.380
And you're going to be in there for a minute.

1197
00:56:55.580 --> 00:57:01.640
Every time they would announce Jenna is 10-1, I would get a lot of anxiety because I would

1198
00:57:01.640 --> 00:57:04.340
think I better make it fast or they're going to think I'm doing a poop.

1199
00:57:04.740 --> 00:57:05.180
Right.

1200
00:57:05.180 --> 00:57:07.620
What about when we both started pumping?

1201
00:57:08.040 --> 00:57:10.420
And then you would hear in the walkie, Angela's pumping.

1202
00:57:10.780 --> 00:57:11.900
Angela's going to pump.

1203
00:57:12.400 --> 00:57:12.760
Yeah.

1204
00:57:12.940 --> 00:57:14.060
All your business.

1205
00:57:14.320 --> 00:57:16.840
All your business through a walkie-talkie.

1206
00:57:17.040 --> 00:57:18.240
It's crazy.

1207
00:57:18.460 --> 00:57:21.240
And I get why they have to do it, but it is very surreal.

1208
00:57:21.480 --> 00:57:26.240
And so that clip from Paul really, Jenna and I immediately called each other.

1209
00:57:26.240 --> 00:57:27.160
They were like, oh, my gosh.

1210
00:57:27.580 --> 00:57:29.940
He talked about the thingy with the tracking.

1211
00:57:30.260 --> 00:57:30.820
The tracking.

1212
00:57:32.280 --> 00:57:34.860
They should warn you in film school.

1213
00:57:35.080 --> 00:57:37.060
That you're going to be tracked all day.

1214
00:57:37.180 --> 00:57:39.820
They should warn you because it's very jarring at firSt.

1215
00:57:40.100 --> 00:57:40.300
Yeah.

1216
00:57:40.780 --> 00:57:45.500
Well, let's just say that this meeting of the five families goes very well.

1217
00:57:45.580 --> 00:57:47.420
It also goes very quickly.

1218
00:57:47.420 --> 00:57:49.360
In about three sentences, W.B.

1219
00:57:49.460 --> 00:57:50.300
Jones agrees.

1220
00:57:50.840 --> 00:57:51.360
Fine.

1221
00:57:51.480 --> 00:57:53.020
You can have your parking spaces back.

1222
00:57:53.180 --> 00:57:53.700
It's a triumph.

1223
00:57:53.700 --> 00:57:56.680
Listen, it looks like Kevin's about to cry.

1224
00:57:56.820 --> 00:57:58.520
And Bill Kress is not here for that.

1225
00:57:58.720 --> 00:58:01.760
He can't handle a grown man crying about a parking spot.

1226
00:58:01.940 --> 00:58:03.540
And so Paul Faust calls it.

1227
00:58:03.660 --> 00:58:05.180
He's like, just give them their spots back.

1228
00:58:05.340 --> 00:58:07.040
And it just wraps up so quickly.

1229
00:58:07.280 --> 00:58:07.880
It does.

1230
00:58:08.040 --> 00:58:10.840
And then Paul has a great line where he says this could have been an email.

1231
00:58:11.140 --> 00:58:12.340
Which is like a meme.

1232
00:58:12.520 --> 00:58:13.820
It also speaks to me.

1233
00:58:14.320 --> 00:58:14.500
Yeah.

1234
00:58:14.840 --> 00:58:18.340
I mean, Paul needs to get out and ride his motorcycle.

1235
00:58:18.900 --> 00:58:19.600
He's done.

1236
00:58:20.960 --> 00:58:24.120
Kevin's talking head after this scene broke my heart.

1237
00:58:24.560 --> 00:58:26.340
Brian did such a good job with it.

1238
00:58:26.560 --> 00:58:31.040
He just says that after Stacey broke up with him, it was really a tough time.

1239
00:58:31.280 --> 00:58:34.260
And, well, it's just nice to win one.

1240
00:58:34.500 --> 00:58:35.820
And he tears up.

1241
00:58:35.980 --> 00:58:37.320
It was so sweet.

1242
00:58:37.760 --> 00:58:37.960
Yeah.

1243
00:58:38.420 --> 00:58:44.980
Guys, we didn't go to the character of Kevin very often for a heartfelt moment on the show.

1244
00:58:44.980 --> 00:58:50.740
And it's a shame because Brian Baumgartner is a fantastic actor.

1245
00:58:51.100 --> 00:58:53.800
And you can see that in this talking head.

1246
00:58:54.200 --> 00:58:54.640
Yeah.

1247
00:58:54.780 --> 00:58:58.100
I was excited to get to see this performance from him.

1248
00:58:58.520 --> 00:58:59.380
Great job, Bea.

1249
00:58:59.820 --> 00:59:00.460
Oh, no.

1250
00:59:00.560 --> 00:59:03.600
Michael has come back from his date and he's very angry.

1251
00:59:04.060 --> 00:59:04.560
Mm-hmm.

1252
00:59:04.720 --> 00:59:06.420
He gives a big speech.

1253
00:59:06.920 --> 00:59:09.640
Angela, here are the things that he's a man of.

1254
00:59:10.280 --> 00:59:10.840
Intensity.

1255
00:59:11.140 --> 00:59:11.480
Mm-hmm.

1256
00:59:11.740 --> 00:59:12.180
Cool.

1257
00:59:12.920 --> 00:59:13.180
Youth.

1258
00:59:13.380 --> 00:59:14.460
And passionately.

1259
00:59:15.120 --> 00:59:16.720
He's a man of passionately.

1260
00:59:17.320 --> 00:59:19.860
He's a man of youth and passionately.

1261
00:59:21.420 --> 00:59:26.560
Well, at 15 minutes, 10 seconds, you might notice I'm writing on the top of my desk.

1262
00:59:26.700 --> 00:59:31.700
I have several papers on this very little thin lip of the top of my desk.

1263
00:59:32.080 --> 00:59:38.500
I had to do that for camera because when I was staging my writing on the actual desk,

1264
00:59:38.640 --> 00:59:40.760
they couldn't see my face when Michael entered.

1265
00:59:40.760 --> 00:59:46.160
I have to imagine that was a tough reach and very awkward because you're leaning over your

1266
00:59:46.160 --> 00:59:48.800
desk to write on a tiny ledge.

1267
00:59:49.060 --> 00:59:50.560
Well, I'll tell you something.

1268
00:59:50.780 --> 00:59:54.100
Pam's desk was very narrow in general.

1269
00:59:54.100 --> 01:00:00.820
I couldn't fit more than a keyboard on it in order to write on Pam's actual desk, which

1270
01:00:00.820 --> 01:00:03.560
was made very thin, I think, for production.

1271
01:00:03.560 --> 01:00:05.580
It was always awkward.

1272
01:00:05.840 --> 01:00:06.240
Always.

1273
01:00:06.760 --> 01:00:07.140
Yeah.

1274
01:00:07.380 --> 01:00:10.600
So I've perched up on that little ledge, which was also awkward.

1275
01:00:11.160 --> 01:00:15.900
In this scene, Jenna, it seems like Jim and Pam are just kind of teasing each other.

1276
01:00:16.080 --> 01:00:19.360
Like, he's like, what were you thinking setting Michael up with your landlord?

1277
01:00:19.640 --> 01:00:20.920
Now you have to move out.

1278
01:00:21.140 --> 01:00:21.940
That went horribly.

1279
01:00:22.420 --> 01:00:23.640
And they're kind of joking.

1280
01:00:23.740 --> 01:00:25.980
And she's like, maybe I should move in with my boyfriend.

1281
01:00:26.440 --> 01:00:28.840
And then Pam has a moment of vulnerability.

1282
01:00:28.840 --> 01:00:33.960
And you see it in your face, Jenna, where you're going to be an advocate for yourself.

1283
01:00:34.180 --> 01:00:35.720
This is something you've been working on.

1284
01:00:36.160 --> 01:00:37.340
And she says, you know what?

1285
01:00:37.380 --> 01:00:40.580
I'm only going to move in with my boyfriend if I'm engaged.

1286
01:00:41.060 --> 01:00:41.440
Yeah.

1287
01:00:41.800 --> 01:00:43.360
And Jim doesn't miss a beat.

1288
01:00:43.580 --> 01:00:44.680
He's like, oh, it's coming.

1289
01:00:45.020 --> 01:00:45.740
Of course.

1290
01:00:46.180 --> 01:00:49.700
And then Pam just kind of keeps joking along.

1291
01:00:49.920 --> 01:00:51.660
And Jenna, I was like, does Pam?

1292
01:00:51.660 --> 01:00:55.760
I mean, in the moment, I was positive that Jim was serious.

1293
01:00:56.200 --> 01:00:57.280
Does Pam know?

1294
01:00:57.680 --> 01:00:59.380
Does Pam know he's doing a bit?

1295
01:00:59.560 --> 01:01:01.620
Does it hit her that he's not?

1296
01:01:02.140 --> 01:01:10.980
I think this maybe, in my backstory, was kind of one of the first times that this idea of

1297
01:01:10.980 --> 01:01:14.180
them getting engaged or married has really come up.

1298
01:01:14.880 --> 01:01:21.200
And that was a reason why I chose to kind of hesitate as well when Pam says, oh, I'm not

1299
01:01:21.200 --> 01:01:27.000
going to move in with anyone until I'm engaged, is because this idea of being engaged, it's

1300
01:01:27.000 --> 01:01:30.960
not something that I thought they had talked about a lot yet.

1301
01:01:31.320 --> 01:01:31.440
No.

1302
01:01:31.560 --> 01:01:33.200
And that's how it reads.

1303
01:01:33.200 --> 01:01:38.480
As an audience, I loved it because this is how real life stuff comes up.

1304
01:01:38.620 --> 01:01:41.820
Sometimes it just seeps in to like a mundane conversation.

1305
01:01:41.820 --> 01:01:43.920
And then you're like, oh, crap, we're here now.

1306
01:01:44.100 --> 01:01:44.520
OK.

1307
01:01:45.100 --> 01:01:45.440
Yes.

1308
01:01:45.840 --> 01:01:49.900
And so I think Pam isn't totally sure what to do with this conversation.

1309
01:01:49.900 --> 01:01:51.160
Was it a bit?

1310
01:01:51.320 --> 01:01:52.180
Is he being serious?

1311
01:01:52.680 --> 01:01:58.760
Now, he has a talking head for the audience where he says, oh, I'm not kidding.

1312
01:01:59.120 --> 01:02:02.140
I got this a week after we started dating.

1313
01:02:02.400 --> 01:02:04.120
And he shows an engagement ring.

1314
01:02:04.620 --> 01:02:07.900
And I'm sorry, does he carry it to work with him every day?

1315
01:02:08.180 --> 01:02:09.180
I don't know.

1316
01:02:09.340 --> 01:02:10.500
Does it live in his pocket?

1317
01:02:10.720 --> 01:02:13.000
Is he too worried to leave it at his apartment?

1318
01:02:13.320 --> 01:02:16.100
Like, he has it ready to go.

1319
01:02:16.100 --> 01:02:22.620
Oh, we had a fan question from Abigail B who said, is the ring Jim shows the camera, the

1320
01:02:22.620 --> 01:02:27.180
ring he proposes with later and the one that Pam wears for the rest of the show?

1321
01:02:27.940 --> 01:02:29.520
I think it is.

1322
01:02:30.180 --> 01:02:31.720
I think that it is.

1323
01:02:31.720 --> 01:02:37.340
I remember Phil Shea coming up to me and presenting me with a tray of rings.

1324
01:02:37.720 --> 01:02:44.480
And he and I had a very long conversation about what kind of ring would Jim buy Pam.

1325
01:02:44.960 --> 01:02:47.400
Like, Jim's going to hit it out of the park for Pam.

1326
01:02:47.620 --> 01:02:50.740
Remember, Pam was always a little disappointed in her ring from Roy.

1327
01:02:50.880 --> 01:02:51.880
That was my backstory.

1328
01:02:52.160 --> 01:02:52.840
The cluster.

1329
01:02:53.240 --> 01:02:53.640
Yes.

1330
01:02:53.640 --> 01:02:58.780
And so Phil was like, what would make Pam's heart sing?

1331
01:02:59.440 --> 01:03:01.380
And I remember picking that ring.

1332
01:03:02.200 --> 01:03:04.540
So, yeah, I think it is that ring.

1333
01:03:04.960 --> 01:03:06.420
Pretty bold move, Jim.

1334
01:03:06.820 --> 01:03:09.500
Buy it the first week you guys started dating.

1335
01:03:09.980 --> 01:03:10.140
Yeah.

1336
01:03:10.240 --> 01:03:12.400
But I guess he knew he loved her for a long time.

1337
01:03:12.800 --> 01:03:13.040
Yeah.

1338
01:03:13.280 --> 01:03:17.100
Well, guys, here's the scene that's in Michael's office that I love.

1339
01:03:17.220 --> 01:03:18.460
It's shot a little differently.

1340
01:03:18.680 --> 01:03:19.760
This was Jeff Blitz.

1341
01:03:19.760 --> 01:03:25.700
You know, there's always so many of the same scenes in the same places because we only had

1342
01:03:25.700 --> 01:03:29.820
one set and every once in a while, a director would find a fun way to shoot.

1343
01:03:30.040 --> 01:03:34.660
And Jeff Blitz had this idea for this scene between Michael and Dwight that he would just

1344
01:03:34.660 --> 01:03:42.580
lower the camera a little and shoot at an angle and slowly push in as Dwight convinces

1345
01:03:42.580 --> 01:03:45.380
Michael that what he needs is closure.

1346
01:03:45.740 --> 01:03:49.120
And when I watched the scene, there was a brief moment.

1347
01:03:49.760 --> 01:03:52.080
That I thought he meant with Jan.

1348
01:03:52.320 --> 01:03:52.700
Same.

1349
01:03:52.880 --> 01:03:54.720
He needs closure with Jan.

1350
01:03:55.080 --> 01:03:55.880
But no.

1351
01:03:56.160 --> 01:03:56.460
No.

1352
01:03:56.600 --> 01:03:57.800
It's with the chair model.

1353
01:03:58.180 --> 01:04:00.160
So they head to the cemetery.

1354
01:04:00.520 --> 01:04:02.200
Of a woman he's never met.

1355
01:04:02.540 --> 01:04:02.900
Mm-hmm.

1356
01:04:03.300 --> 01:04:04.120
That's right.

1357
01:04:04.320 --> 01:04:13.540
And they are going to launch into a tribute to her by singing a version of American Pie.

1358
01:04:14.080 --> 01:04:14.760
Bye.

1359
01:04:15.000 --> 01:04:17.320
Bye, Miss Chair Model Lady.

1360
01:04:17.760 --> 01:04:21.990
Oh, my gosh.

1361
01:04:21.990 --> 01:04:26.050
So, you know, the writers actually had a hard time getting a song cleared for this.

1362
01:04:26.610 --> 01:04:27.130
Shocker.

1363
01:04:27.270 --> 01:04:32.710
The first song they were going to parody was Elton John's Candle in the Wind.

1364
01:04:33.050 --> 01:04:38.810
But they could not get the rights because, here's an interesting bit of trivia, he had

1365
01:04:38.810 --> 01:04:44.030
given permission for the song to be parodied on 30 Rock and he did not care for the results.

1366
01:04:44.030 --> 01:04:47.390
So he said no more parodies of Candle in the Wind.

1367
01:04:47.630 --> 01:04:47.970
No.

1368
01:04:47.970 --> 01:04:48.670
Okay.

1369
01:04:49.010 --> 01:04:52.670
They also wrote parodies of Legs by ZZ Top.

1370
01:04:53.070 --> 01:04:54.770
She's got legs.

1371
01:04:54.950 --> 01:04:57.770
How is that a song you sing at a gravesite?

1372
01:04:57.970 --> 01:05:01.190
She was a chair model and you really see her legs in the picture.

1373
01:05:01.490 --> 01:05:02.690
Oh, my gosh.

1374
01:05:02.690 --> 01:05:03.250
Okay.

1375
01:05:03.250 --> 01:05:06.770
And then also Ruby Tuesday by the Rolling Stones.

1376
01:05:07.110 --> 01:05:08.770
Oh, I love Ruby Tuesday.

1377
01:05:09.090 --> 01:05:11.430
But the song that got cleared was American Pie.

1378
01:05:11.630 --> 01:05:17.010
And I'm so glad it did because I really love them in the dark singing.

1379
01:05:17.010 --> 01:05:22.190
Well, we can't wrap up this episode without this adorable scene.

1380
01:05:22.470 --> 01:05:25.950
Jenna, I literally wrote adorable and highlighted it.

1381
01:05:26.050 --> 01:05:26.470
Mm-hmm.

1382
01:05:26.910 --> 01:05:30.710
Between Jim and Pam as they're walking to the satellite parking lot.

1383
01:05:31.190 --> 01:05:32.970
And the sun is setting.

1384
01:05:33.210 --> 01:05:35.350
You guys both look like angels.

1385
01:05:35.750 --> 01:05:38.090
You both look so flippin' adorable.

1386
01:05:38.590 --> 01:05:41.550
Well, I remember we had to wait in our trailer for that light.

1387
01:05:41.850 --> 01:05:43.510
That's called the magic hour.

1388
01:05:43.710 --> 01:05:44.110
Yeah.

1389
01:05:44.270 --> 01:05:44.950
In the biz.

1390
01:05:45.170 --> 01:05:49.350
And there's this little 45 minutes when the sun is truly magical.

1391
01:05:50.130 --> 01:05:52.370
Everybody, take your photos at magic hour.

1392
01:05:52.530 --> 01:05:53.690
It's when you look the beSt.

1393
01:05:53.850 --> 01:05:54.310
That's right.

1394
01:05:54.310 --> 01:05:57.750
If you're planning a wedding, plan for some, like, magic hour photos.

1395
01:05:58.250 --> 01:05:58.430
Yeah.

1396
01:05:58.690 --> 01:06:01.490
So we waited for that moment.

1397
01:06:01.490 --> 01:06:04.610
And we could only do a few takes because then we were going to lose the light.

1398
01:06:04.870 --> 01:06:06.610
He drops to one knee.

1399
01:06:07.090 --> 01:06:07.430
Mm-hmm.

1400
01:06:07.890 --> 01:06:13.290
Well, I'll tell you, this moment was promo'd like crazy.

1401
01:06:13.650 --> 01:06:14.710
Like crazy.

1402
01:06:15.030 --> 01:06:16.710
I bet they blasted it out.

1403
01:06:16.990 --> 01:06:17.510
Yeah.

1404
01:06:17.790 --> 01:06:22.810
Well, you know, we talked about how season four was interrupted by the writer's strike.

1405
01:06:22.810 --> 01:06:29.510
Like, the writers originally had this very, very big storyline for Jim and Pam that I think

1406
01:06:29.510 --> 01:06:32.670
involved them moving in together, if I'm correct.

1407
01:06:33.190 --> 01:06:38.970
And instead, because we lost a bunch of episodes, they were like, you know what?

1408
01:06:38.970 --> 01:06:41.710
We should skip that and they should just get engaged.

1409
01:06:41.710 --> 01:06:46.530
Or we should just skip to the part where we're thinking they might get engaged any minute.

1410
01:06:46.830 --> 01:06:50.410
And so that was where this part of the story came from.

1411
01:06:50.870 --> 01:06:51.670
I like it.

1412
01:06:51.670 --> 01:06:53.790
I like that Pam still had her own place.

1413
01:06:54.130 --> 01:06:54.470
Mm-hmm.

1414
01:06:54.570 --> 01:06:59.350
I like all of that storyline for her because you sort of feel like Pam lived with Roy for

1415
01:06:59.350 --> 01:07:00.410
a long time.

1416
01:07:00.810 --> 01:07:04.250
And then she finally carved out her own space in the world.

1417
01:07:04.290 --> 01:07:06.770
And I really liked that choice.

1418
01:07:07.050 --> 01:07:10.970
Well, your reaction when you realize that Jim is faking is so great.

1419
01:07:11.130 --> 01:07:11.930
It's so cute.

1420
01:07:12.090 --> 01:07:12.970
You're like, I hate you.

1421
01:07:13.130 --> 01:07:14.030
And he's like, what?

1422
01:07:14.090 --> 01:07:15.330
It's really cute.

1423
01:07:15.830 --> 01:07:16.150
Yeah.

1424
01:07:16.150 --> 01:07:18.250
I have some bad news.

1425
01:07:18.890 --> 01:07:19.150
What?

1426
01:07:19.510 --> 01:07:22.790
I mean, spoiler alert, Pam's going to need to go to art school.

1427
01:07:23.190 --> 01:07:24.690
We're going to frustrate you.

1428
01:07:24.910 --> 01:07:25.530
I know.

1429
01:07:25.790 --> 01:07:29.390
This proposal's going to take a little while, but we're planting the seeds.

1430
01:07:29.670 --> 01:07:32.510
They have to pull you guys apart to put you back together.

1431
01:07:32.690 --> 01:07:33.610
It makes me crazy.

1432
01:07:34.090 --> 01:07:34.970
I know.

1433
01:07:34.970 --> 01:07:38.430
Well, guys, that is Chair Model.

1434
01:07:38.950 --> 01:07:40.830
Next up is Night Out.

1435
01:07:41.490 --> 01:07:44.890
I'm really enjoying season four, Angela, I have to say.

1436
01:07:45.310 --> 01:07:46.070
Me too.

1437
01:07:46.450 --> 01:07:47.550
So fun.

1438
01:07:47.870 --> 01:07:52.250
And for all of you chair models out there, just know your work is not in vain.

1439
01:07:52.470 --> 01:07:55.790
You might be inspiring others to find true love.

1440
01:07:56.310 --> 01:08:03.470
And Paul, thank you so much for sending in these audio clips and being so quick to respond

1441
01:08:03.470 --> 01:08:04.450
when I texted you.

1442
01:08:04.630 --> 01:08:06.250
You are just such a delight.

1443
01:08:06.550 --> 01:08:07.850
And I adore you.

1444
01:08:08.050 --> 01:08:09.490
And these made our day, Jenna.

1445
01:08:09.690 --> 01:08:12.650
And you guys, 1-800-PREPARE.com.

1446
01:08:13.030 --> 01:08:17.210
Get your preparedness, safety, and survival information there and all the kits you might

1447
01:08:17.210 --> 01:08:17.770
ever need.

1448
01:08:18.050 --> 01:08:19.390
You know I'm on that.

1449
01:08:19.750 --> 01:08:20.910
Oh, I know.

1450
01:08:21.070 --> 01:08:23.270
I was like, oh, Paul, there's some good stuff on here.

1451
01:08:23.350 --> 01:08:24.750
Christmas gift for me.

1452
01:08:24.930 --> 01:08:25.630
Earmark that.

1453
01:08:26.830 --> 01:08:29.310
Survival and emergency kits and products.

1454
01:08:29.470 --> 01:08:29.670
Jenna.

1455
01:08:30.270 --> 01:08:31.850
We'll see you next week, everyone.

1456
01:08:32.110 --> 01:08:33.050
See you next week.

